<?nvs
// The file a mount runs, as a program.
//
// `rule:http-server/a-request-resolves-in-five-steps`'s
// five steps all end in the same place: step 5 runs **the mount's entry**, and
// this is one. Nothing here derives that path. § 2 is the server's governing
// rule — a request *selects* an entry point from a set enumerated before it
// arrived and may never construct one — so the table this file is in was
// expanded against disk at boot and no line below could route to another file
// even if it wanted to.
//
// The same file runs under `nvs run` and under `nvs serve`, because of
// `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink`:
// `echo` always has a sink and the *context* attaches it. Under `nvs run`
// that is the terminal and these lines are stdout; under `nvs serve` it is the
// response body, carried by `Core\Html\Markup` and auto-escaping. The program
// does not branch on which, and there is no member here that would have to.

// A body is text this program built. The server fills no variable for you,
// so every value here was written by this program.
string $body = 'novis' . ' ' . 'serves this file';
echo $body, "\n";

// One entry answers whatever its mount was given, which is why the mount
// table is a table of prefixes and not a table of files: the second line is the
// same line for every path this mount matched.
echo 'entry=examples/serve.nvs', "\n";
